                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Overall? I'd say I recommend it, IF you meet any of the following criteria:\n[list]\n[*] You are desperate for games with socialist messaging\n[*] You have a consistent flow of disposable income \n[/list]\n\nThe game was rather short, I got 3 cogs on all levels and got all achievements in 5 hours. $15 is a lot to ask for such a short game. As a filthy labor aristocrat (Marxist term for middle-class laborer), I don't have to worry about spending the occasional $15 every so often. But usually when its around this price range its something GOOD. Think Pizza Tower, Plague Inc, Loop Hero, Dead cells, and if you wanna talk about socialist games, Cruelty Squad. Tonight we Riot, on the other hand, is a generally unpolished game with only 21 levels in it. There is VERY little to do in this game. It's a fun short experience, but the price is just far too much for a game of this scale. The weapon variety is fun, the bosses are interesting, the visual design is great, but again, its really short.\n\nThere is another part to this game though, something that potentially could have added replayability. It's the endless mode. Unfortunately, the game literally tells you about 10 minutes into the endless mode that this is about as far as the mode was actually tested and it pretty much starts fucking with you directly afterwards. It turns into a developer commentary kinda, it talks about the pandemic, and then it gives you an achievement before sending in helicopters that bomb you and instantly kill you. So no, its not an endless mode. It's a 55 wave mission. Its fun, no doubt, but its not endless. Unless you figure out how to get past the helicopters, I'll try that later.\n\nI don't regret purchasing the game, but I found myself quite disappointed in it. There's a lot more this game could have been. I was really hoping you would get to manage the production in some way, or it would talk about the more complicated parts of revolution, or maybe even talk about proletarian internationalism, but it doesn't do any of those things. It's a fine game, fun even. But it just leaves a lot to be desired, and again, $15 is a lot for a working class person to spend on something so lacking in content. The truly important thing about art is its ability to provide us with new perspectives, and I honestly don't think this game does enough to actually make the player consider these ideas if they weren't already a commie.",
